<div className='hidden'>

## Guides

### Your first menu

Everything starts off with the root component -- it must wrap the whole menu:

```tsx
<DropdownMenu.Root>
  {/* ... */}
</DropdownMenu.Root>
```

First, let's render the menu trigger, which opens the menu when pressed:

```tsx
<DropdownMenu.Root>
  <DropdownMenu.Trigger>
    Click me!
  <DropdownMenu.Trigger>
</DropdownMenu.Root>
```

We can render a custom element in place of the default `<button>` using the `render` prop.

For example, here we're using the shadcn/ui button with outline variant:

```tsx
<DropdownMenu.Root>
  <DropdownMenu.Trigger render={<Button variant="outline" />}>
    Click me!
  <DropdownMenu.Trigger>
</DropdownMenu.Root>
```

<br />

Next, let's render the actual menu popup.

- `Portal` moves the popup to a different part of the DOM; by default, it is appended to `<body>`.
- `Positioner` positions the popup against the trigger.
- `Popup` renders the actual popup container.

Optionally, we can also use `Overlay` and `Arrow`... but we'll omit these for this basic example.

```tsx
<DropdownMenu.Root>
  {/* ...  */}
  <DropdownMenu.Portal>
    <DropdownMenu.Positioner>
      <DropdownMenu.Popup>
        {/* ... */}
      </DropdownMenu.Popup>
    </DropdownMenu.Positioner>
  </DropdownMenu.Portal>
</DropdownMenu.Root>
```

<br />

Within the menu popup, we can render our actual menu surface (via `DropdownMenu.Surface`), which handles the core listbox logic for us.

A menu surface usually contains:

- `DropdownMenu.Input` to search/filter the menu items.
- `DropdownMenu.List` to wrap our menu rows.
- `DropdownMenu.Empty` automatically renders when there are no items matching the search query; to be rendered inside `DropdownMenu.List`.


```tsx
<DropdownMenu.Surface>
  <DropdownMenu.Input />
  <DropdownMenu.List>
    <DropdownMenu.Empty>
      No matching results.
    </DropdownMenu.Empty>
  </DropdownMenu.List>
</DropdownMenu.Surface>
```

If you don't want the input to be visible, we recommend rendering the input with the `hideUntilActive` prop, hiding the input until the user starts typing:

<Example name="guides/dropdown-menu/your-first-menu/surface-hidden-input">
  <Example.PreviewComponent />
  <Example.PreviewCode>

```tsx
<DropdownMenu.Input hideUntilActive />
```

  </Example.PreviewCode>
</Example>



<br />

Let's add some items to this menu. We'll use the `onSelect` prop to configure *what* happens when you select an item:

<Example name="guides/dropdown-menu/your-first-menu/items-02">
  <Example.PreviewComponent />
  <Example.PreviewCode>

```tsx
<DropdownMenu.List>
  <DropdownMenu.Item onSelect={() => toast('Apple')}>
    Apple
  </DropdownMenu.Item>
  <DropdownMenu.Item onSelect={() => toast('Banana')}>
    Banana
  </DropdownMenu.Item>
  <DropdownMenu.Item onSelect={() => toast('Orange')}>
    Orange
  </DropdownMenu.Item>
</DropdownMenu.List>
```

  </Example.PreviewCode>
</Example>

</div>
